# Control slider

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Use a slider to adjust a known, bounded value such as brightness or volume. Pair it with a visible value and, when the task needs an exact value, a typed field and visible step buttons. All of them change one draft. Keep the last reported device value separate.

Choose the right control:

| Task | Use | Do not assume |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Adjust brightness or volume approximately | A slider with a visible value and an exact alternative | That 0% means off or mute for every integration |
| Set an exact temperature or offset | Typed entry and step buttons; a slider may supplement them | A universal range, step, unit or safe setting |
| Choose a named mode | Radios, a [select](select.html) or a [Control select](control-select.html) | A numeric sequence between unrelated modes |
| Show a measurement | Text, a gauge or a chart with its unit and date | An editable slider only because the value is a number |
| Move a cover, valve or other mechanism | The [access and movement guidance](../home-assistant/access-and-movement.html) and the supported position control | That moving a draft thumb is harmless or confirms movement |
| Set two related bounds | Two labelled exact fields with ordering checks | That two overlapping thumbs are accessible |

Live updates, permissions, physical actions and reconciliation are owned by [Continuous controls and alternatives](../home-assistant/continuous-controls.html).

This page covers:

- Control slider
  - `<input type="range">`
  - `ha-slider`
  - `ha-control-slider`
  - `ha-grid-layout-slider`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A device heading, and a separately labelled reported value.
- A draft label, with the minimum, maximum, step and unit written out.
- The slider: a track, a round thumb and often a tooltip with the value.
- Exact entry and decrease and increase buttons, when precision matters.
- An action to apply the draft.

State the unit in the labels. Do not rely on a coloured track or a suffix alone. Keep the thumb round so it reads as something that moves, with a control area at least 44 CSS pixels high and room for the focus ring. Do not use red and green along an ordinary range to imply danger or success. Respect forced colours.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-input-type-range"><code>&lt;input type="range"&gt;</code></h3>

The HTML slider, used on Civica documentation pages.

- `min` (0 by default), `max` (100), `step` (1, or `any`) and `value` (the midpoint by default). `list` can add tick marks, but support varies.
- It always has a value. It cannot say “no value”, so hide it when a device has no reading.
- It has a built-in slider role. Do not add a second `role="slider"` or a duplicate `aria-valuenow`.
- It needs a visible label. Use `aria-valuetext` when the number alone is unclear, for example “40%”.
- It has no text output. Pair it with an `output` element or a typed field.
- Arrow keys, Home and End work in every browser. Page Up and Page Down depend on the browser.
- The browser draws it by default, and Civica restyles it with a round thumb and ink border.

Make the typed field a text field so a minus sign stays available on phone keypads. Check the value against the range and step, keep what was typed when it is wrong, and explain the limit.

<h3 id="child-ha-slider"><code>ha-slider</code></h3>

The small Home Assistant slider, in two sizes (`s`, the default, and `m`) with an optional tooltip. People see it as the number selector in slider mode, in forms, and in entity rows for a `number` entity. There is no card type for it.

- Thumb 16 pixels (`s`) or 20 pixels (`m`). The touch area is larger, at 32 pixels.
- The thumb and filled track take `primary-color`. The track uses Home Assistant’s default disabled colour, which the Civica theme does not set.
- Focus uses the theme’s focus ring, so the Civica focus colours reach it.
- Do not rely on `slider-color` or `slider-track-color` in the theme to reach it.

<h3 id="child-ha-control-slider"><code>ha-control-slider</code></h3>

The large slider in more-info dialogs and card features, vertical or horizontal. It appears for light brightness, cover position, valve position, fan speed, target temperature, target humidity, a number and media volume.

```yaml
features:
  - type: light-brightness
```

```yaml
features:
  - type: numeric-input
    style: slider
```

The `target-temperature` and `target-humidity` features also take `style: slider`.

- Keys: arrows step, Page Up and Page Down move a tenth of the range, Home and End go to the ends. A tap sets a value, and dragging needs to move 10 pixels first.
- It is exposed as a slider with the label as its name and the value and unit in words. The tooltip is hidden from assistive technology because the value is already spoken.
- The fill takes `primary-color`. The track uses the default disabled colour at low opacity.
- Focus is a 2 pixel ring in the primary colour. The Civica yellow focus ring does not apply. Changing it, or the thickness, needs the card-mod integration.

<h3 id="child-ha-grid-layout-slider"><code>ha-grid-layout-slider</code></h3>

An editor-only slider. It appears in the dashboard editor when you choose how many columns and rows a card takes in a sections view. People who only use dashboards never see it.

- Range 1 to 4 by default, with a dot for each step.
- It is exposed as a slider, but it does not set its own name. So do not claim it is labelled.
- It uses `primary-color`, the card colours and a 2 pixel primary-colour focus ring.

Home Assistant sliders do not accept typed values. For exact entry, use the number selector or an entity row in a mode that offers a text box, where the integration offers it, or give people a form with exact entry and step buttons.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A slider is fast for an approximate value, and the thumb shows where the value sits in its range.
- It is slow and fiddly for an exact value, and it needs a steady pointer.
- A native range cannot show “no value”.
- Home Assistant sliders cannot be replaced with Civica markup. The theme sets colours, and the other settings need card-mod.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Show the number and its unit outside the thumb.
- Write the minimum, maximum and step.
- Offer exact entry and step buttons, so people who cannot drag still have a route.
- Do not send a command on every pixel moved.
- Keep a draft separate from a reported value, and say when a request was sent, accepted or failed.
- Never present a thumb position as a reading when the device is unavailable.
- Check the form at 320 CSS pixels, with step buttons wrapping.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: every slider can be moved with the keyboard, and `ha-slider` and `ha-control-slider` also take a tap. Offer step buttons where you can.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the `ha-slider` touch area is 32 pixels.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: the tracks use Home Assistant’s default colours, so measure them on your dashboard.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (AAA): the ring differs between sliders.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, 1.3.1 and 3.3.2: a label, a role and a value in words.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: it works at 320 CSS pixels.

