<h2 id="state-and-failure">State and failure behaviour</h2>

| State | Required behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| Valid reported value | Show the unit and keep the real minimum, maximum and step |
| Editing a valid draft | Keep the slider, typed field and step buttons in step, without applying |
| Empty, non-numeric, out-of-range or off-step text | Keep the typed text, explain the limit, leave the last valid thumb position and prevent applying |
| At an endpoint | Stop at the bound and never wrap from maximum to minimum |
| Request pending | Prevent a duplicate for the same target and keep both the requested and the earlier reported value |
| Request accepted | Say it was accepted, and wait for a reported reading before saying the device changed |
| Request failed or timed out | Keep the draft and the latest reading, and offer a deliberate retry |
| Unknown or unavailable | Say why it cannot be adjusted and do not invent a number |
| Range, permissions or units change during editing | Check the draft again and explain the new limit before sending |
| Another person or automation changes the device | Show the new reading and do not overwrite an edit in progress |

<h2 id="keyboard-and-touch">Keyboard and touch</h2>

Native ranges support the arrow keys, Home and End. Keep focus visible, keep the label outside the moving thumb, and make every alternative work without a pointer. Test with a screen reader on a real touch device before you make an accessibility claim.

<h2 id="slider-acceptance">Acceptance steps</h2>

1. Adjust each example with the arrows, the ends, exact text and step buttons. Check that all draft displays agree and the reported text does not change.
2. Check empty text, a minus sign being typed, decimal steps, both ends, out-of-range and off-step values. Invalid text must not be replaced or sent.
3. Check that an accepted response looks different from a failure and that neither implies a physical result.
4. Use only the keyboard, a 320 pixel layout, 200% text, forced colours and a touch screen reader. Check labels, units, focus and errors.
5. For a real dashboard, record the Home Assistant version, what the integration supports, permission changes, remote updates and disconnection.

See [Native task accessibility and evidence](../home-assistant/native-task-accessibility.html).
